This stream contained one demo in which gaps between aligned markers were colored, another where the same kind of candidate rule was not evident, an identity example featuring a closed rectangular outline, and a test with several tempting but structurally different boundary patterns. Two speculative fill submissions failed. The durable lesson is to separate (1) whether the geometry is aligned/enclosed, (2) what configuration triggers action, and (3) which exact cells are in scope. An identity example is strong negative evidence against indiscriminately filling every enclosure or same-row/column gap.

After a rejected fill, reconstruct all demo diffs and explicitly compare gap-filling, enclosure-filling, and identity-preservation hypotheses against every pair. Do not infer the correct mask from a prior session note; verify the current grid's boundaries, coordinates, and dimensions. Spend another demo only after naming competing hypotheses and the specific outcome that would distinguish them. Treat a previous session's noted correction as a candidate to verify, never as a substitute for current-instance evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u076/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md", "content": "# Periodic output versus input-driven transformation

When several examples show periodic full-grid outputs, first verify whether the output is generated solely from a fixed palette cycle or whether it is a transformation of the input's marker arrangement. Record the complete mapping from each input row/cell to the corresponding output, including locations where the input is blank and output is nonblank. A small demo with distinctive sparse markers can reveal an operation such as filling a sequence, shifting or cycling colors, or extending a pattern; do not ignore it because other examples are dense stripes.

After a periodic-pattern submission fails, reject that generator as a whole unless it can be reconciled with every demo. Do not spend further submissions on arbitrary phase or palette variants. Use remaining demos to resolve explicit alternatives (e.g. row-wise versus column-wise sequence, marker-order preservation versus fixed cycle), then validate the candidate on all known examples before resubmitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u076/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md"}

